// GENERATED by scripts/unseen-build-bank.ts from resources/english-unseen/bank/*.json. Do not edit by hand:
// change the bank file and run `npm run unseen-build-bank`.
// SERVER ONLY: the marker's written description of each visual text (never sent to a browser).
// SERVER ONLY: never import this file from a client component or a public module.

export const descriptions: Record<string, string> = {
  "fs23-t1": "A black-and-white line drawing, seen from above at an angle, of a round banquet table crowded with plates, bowls, glasses, bottles (one a Coca-Cola bottle), spoons and chopsticks, laid out like a shared Chinese meal. The diners are shown only in part, cut off by the frame: arms in shirtsleeves and dark suits reach in with chopsticks, a hand wearing a wristwatch, a hand gripping a bottle by its neck. Their faces are never shown.\n\nOn the plates in the middle of the table, instead of food, are tiny human figures, each on its own plate and each living a different moment: a masked woman in a white dress raising one arm with an open hand (it can read as a wave, a call for help or a celebration); behind her, a figure with both arms raised as if cheering; a couple embracing and kissing while wearing face masks; a person walking under an umbrella; a small figure in a white coat (perhaps a doctor or health worker) standing alone at the edge of a plate. One diner's chopsticks reach towards the masked woman as if about to pick her up and eat her.\n\nThe centre of the table is the most detailed part of the image and draws the eye, but no single figure dominates. Solid black areas (the suits, the bottles, a phone lying at the bottom right) contrast with the white tablecloth and the white figures. The artist's signature is at the bottom left.",
  "sgh24-t5": "A portrait-format painting in an oil-paint style with visible brush strokes. The foreground is a grassy hilltop in warm, low golden light; behind and below it is a wide, hazy view of a town or city under a cloudy sky of lilac, mauve and white. A group of odd creatures and one human girl sit on the hilltop, and every one of them is reading a book.\n\nThe largest figure fills the left of the picture. It is a huge, plump, round, brown, owl-like creature with a single big yellow eye with a blue pupil, two small horns, small striped wings on its back and a long thin tail curling along the ground behind it. It sits on a red rug and holds an open book in one hand, its eye turned down towards the page. A tiny winged owl-like creature perches on its head, also holding an open book.\n\nIn front of it, a smaller fox-like creature with long, pointed pink ears like scissor blades and red eyes sits on the rug holding a book up to its face. To its right a dark green, crocodile-like creature with sharp teeth sits inside a cardboard box marked with an upward arrow and holds an open book. In the centre foreground a human girl with long dark hair and a light shirt lies on her stomach on the grass, reading a red book with her feet in the air. To her right a yellow-orange creature with a water tap for a head and a long curling tail sits holding a book beside a tower of stacked books topped by a steaming cup; a small teapot stands in the grass in front. At the lower left a tiny creature with a rounded helmet-like head and a glowing eye stands on a large open book, with other books lying open on the grass around it.\n\nBehind and below the hill, the town is drawn in soft grey-purple rooftops with winding roads and a highway, and several tall chimneys send smoke into the sky. The artist's signature 'TAN 04' is at the bottom right of the painting. The credit printed on the page beneath the picture reads SHAUN TAN, Reading Group.\n\nColour and composition: the foreground is warm yellow, gold and orange from the low sun, while the distant town and sky are cool, hazy mauve and grey. The creatures are clustered close together in one group, all facing their books, and none looks at the town or at the viewer. The large owl-like creature is the biggest shape and anchors the left of the group, with the smaller figures stepping down in size around and in front of it. The horizon sits high in the picture, so the viewer looks across the group towards the town from the same hilltop.",
  "sb25-t1": "A portrait-format campaign poster on a black background, with coloured blocks of yellow, purple, hot pink and pale teal around the edges and large yellow dots at the top left.\n\nTop left: a hot-pink panel with white bold capitals reading 'WE HAVE ALWAYS EXISTED. RADIANT, SOVEREIGN, AND INFINITE, AND IT'S TIME YOU NOT ONLY SEE US BUT LISTEN, LEARN, AND FOLLOW OUR LEAD.' with, in smaller white capitals beneath, 'DYLAN HOSKINS, DUNGHUTTI, BUNDJALUNG, GUMBAYNGGIRR, @ITSHAEDE'.\n\nTop right: a colour photograph of a person with long wavy brown hair and a beard, wearing a silver tiara, drop earrings and a sparkling necklace and an off-the-shoulder black gown, eyes closed and head tilted, arms stretched out to the sides, standing on a high cliff-top above a wide valley with blue sky and white clouds.\n\nBelow: a large black-and-white photograph of a bearded man with curly dark hair, eyes closed, shirtless, with pale markings painted or smeared across his back and shoulder, one arm stretched out to the right and the other across his chest, wearing a dark tulle skirt, standing on a rocky escarpment with a hazy valley and sky behind. Down the left edge of this photograph are three small stacked flags: the Progress Pride flag (rainbow stripes with a chevron of light blue, pink, white, brown and black), the Aboriginal flag (black over red with a yellow circle) and the Torres Strait Islander flag (green, blue and black with a white dancer's headdress).\n\nDown the right edge, a vertical black strip in white bold capitals reads 'STILL HERE STILL QUEER STILL HERE STILL QUEER STILL', with the words 'HERE' and 'QUEER' in heavier type in places.\n\nAt the bottom: a black band holds the circular BLAQ ABORIGINAL CORPORATION logo (a round design in black, white, pink and yellow stripes beside the word 'BLAQ' in pink) and a QR code with small text 'Donate today & continue First Nations LGBTQ+SB advocacy.' A purple strip below reads 'We're building bridges, not barriers. BlaQ Aboriginal Corporation is dedicated to fostering a strong and resilient community where Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander LGBTQ+SB people feel safe, valued, and connected. Visit blaq.org.au'. The caption printed under the picture on the page reads 'BlaQ Aboriginal Corporation, \"Still Here Still Queer\" Campaign'.\n\nMood: both people are calm, dignified and posed on open Country; the bright text and colour blocks are bold and celebratory.",
  "sb24-t1": "A wide colour photograph of the inside of a small theatre, taken from the back of the stalls and looking along the central aisle towards the stage, in dim, low-key lighting. The side walls are dark with a maroon band and a row of warm, glowing wall lamps on each side. Rows of empty maroon theatre seats fill the bottom and the edges of the picture. There are no words in the image.\n\nIn the middle of the stalls, about eleven people sit and stand close together around a pushed-together table, most of them seen from behind or from the side. They are holding open scripts and papers, with pencils and a water bottle on the table, and nearly all of them are looking down at the pages. In the centre a man in a dark green jumper and a woman in a mustard-yellow jumper with a pink scarf sit side by side. To their right a woman with glasses in a red jacket leans over a script. At the right a man in a white shirt and a grey scarf stands holding a script out in one hand while the other hand is raised to his chin. A blonde woman and a woman in a dark top bend over papers at the front right, and a woman with long hair sits at the left. In the lower foreground a man in a dark jacket and a man in a checked shirt, both seen from behind, fill the bottom centre of the picture.\n\nBeyond them is the stage, lit more brightly than the auditorium: a grand piano with a man in a white shirt seated at it, a red rope hung between posts, a few chairs, carrier bags and a plant in a blue pot, with stage machinery and ropes in the dark space behind.\n\nComposition: the group is clustered together in a central band, framed by the empty seats below and the lit stage above. The camera is at about eye height behind the group, so the viewer seems to sit just behind them. No one looks at the camera.",
  "py24-t1": "A landscape-format colour photograph taken indoors, in a room with whitewashed walls, of a group of women gathered around food spread on the floor. The whole back wall behind them is covered by one large rectangular red embroidered textile with a border of small flowers and, across the middle, three large round medallions of flower-like patterns in yellow, pink, teal, purple, white and black on a vivid red ground. At the upper right are the edge of an arched window and a white panelled door; at the upper left a trailing vine with green leaves hangs into the picture.\n\nIn the foreground a blue cloth is spread on the floor and holds the food: stacks of round, flat, golden-brown breads with patterned centres, plastic bags and packets of sweets and white sugary treats, a small cup or teapot and, at the left, a large white bowl of fried pastries into which a woman reaches.\n\nAbout six women sit or kneel around the food in a loose circle, close together. At the far left a woman in a black-and-white zebra-like ikat dress and a maroon and gold headscarf leans in over the bowl, with a woman in a beige headscarf beside her. In the centre background several women in pale floral-print dresses sit behind the food. At the right, a woman in a white dress with large black polka dots and a white headscarf kneels and stretches one end of a long, sheer white cloth across the food, while the woman at the left holds the other end, so the cloth hangs in a shallow curve across the middle of the picture. At the right edge a woman with long dark hair, a yellow-patterned headscarf and a black-and-white ikat dress sits smiling towards the cloth with one hand on her chest.\n\nColour and composition: the vivid red tapestry fills the upper half and frames the group; the blue cloth, the food and the women's black-and-white patterned clothing form a band across the bottom. The women's bodies lean inwards towards the middle, and everyone's hands, eyes and attention are directed at the cloth and the food at the centre. The camera is at about the women's own level. The credit printed below the image reads MATTHIEU PALEY, 2023.",
  "nsg24-t1": "A square digital artwork (a photo-manipulated painting) in rich greens, ochre and browns on the left and a pale, cloudy blue-grey sky on the right, with the sky darkening to smoky grey and black at the right-hand edge. There are no words in the picture.\n\nOn the left, a woman's head and neck are shown in profile, facing right, and are made entirely of landscape. The top and back of her head and her hair are a mountain slope of green grass, rocky outcrops, moss, purple flowers and a small winding path or stream, with roots hanging from the hair at the upper right. Only her face is a human face: a nose, a dark closed-looking eye and bright red lips. A thin white stream runs down her forehead and cheek like a river or a tear and joins a waterfall that pours from her chin down the front of her neck to the bottom of the picture. Small birds or flecks drift in the air around her.\n\nOn the right, a human hand rises from the bottom right corner. The skin is rough and weathered, with glowing orange-red patches on the knuckles like embers. It holds a revolver whose barrel points directly at the woman's mouth, only a short distance from her lips. The top of the barrel and frame is a miniature city: tall skyscrapers, smaller buildings and what look like masts or cranes crowd along it, with a blade-like knife edge beneath the muzzle and a small red tip at the end. Behind the gun the sky is clouded and sooty.\n\nColour and composition: the face-to-barrel confrontation sits at the centre of the picture, with the two sides facing each other horizontally. The living, green, watery side of the picture is large and on the left; the grey, smoky, industrial side with the small hand and the city-gun is on the right.",
  "nsb25-t1": "A portrait-format magazine cover in a flat, graphic, ink-drawn style with fine cross-hatching, mostly in blue-grey and charcoal, with one warm glowing area. At the top, in white capitals, are 'PRICE $9.99' (top left), 'FEB. 10, 2025' (top right) and the masthead THE NEW YORKER ('THE' above 'NEW YORKER'), which overlaps the top of a lit window. The artist's signature 'TOM GAULD' is at the bottom right. A narrow black strip runs down the left edge.\n\nThe scene is a rainy street at dusk or night. Most of the picture is a dark charcoal wall densely hatched with vertical lines for rain, and a wet pavement at the bottom in pale blue-grey with streaks of rain. Cut into the wall in the centre is a large rectangular window, brightly lit in cream, pale pink and orange, set at a slight angle like a stage; it looks into a gallery.\n\nInside the window, a young person with a long brown ponytail, wearing a yellow raincoat, sits with their back to the viewer on a low wooden bench. A small tan dog with a red collar sits beside them on a lead. They are looking at a framed painting on the gallery's back wall, which shows a large round yellow-orange sun above an orange-pink beach, with a tiny figure under a beach umbrella (a summer seaside scene). A small plant or umbrella handle shows at the left of the bench.\n\nOutside in the rain on the pavement: at the lower left a person walks away to the left under a black umbrella, hunched, in a dark coat and shoes; at the right a hooded figure in a long dark coat walks a dark, low, long-bodied dog on a lead, head down. Neither looks into the window.\n\nColour and composition: the glowing warm window is the focal point, surrounded by cold blue-grey; the sun in the painting echoes the warm tones of the room. The people outside face away and are small and dark.",
  "kx25-t4": "A black-and-white wood-engraving or pen-and-ink illustration with dense fine hatching, in landscape format, from 1887. It shows the view from the steerage (cheapest passenger) deck of an ocean steamer as it passes the Statue of Liberty in New York harbour. A faint vertical crease runs down the middle of the image where the page folded.\n\nLeft: the ship's rigging, with ropes and ratlines rising in lines to the top left. Three sailors in caps stand in the rigging at the upper left; the one on the right extends his arm and points across the water to the right.\n\nForeground left and centre: a dense crowd of migrants packs the deck, in layered peasant clothing: headscarves and shawls, fur-trimmed hats, long coats, beards. Many faces are lifted and turned to the right towards the statue. At the front left a man in a fur hat holds his hand to his chest and looks upward; beside him a woman in a patterned headscarf and embroidered bodice looks up and out. Further back a man shades his eyes with his hand to look into the distance, and a woman in a white headscarf gazes outwards. A small child stands on a wooden drum or barrel near the centre, held by an adult. At the bottom centre an elderly woman in a dark headscarf sits low in the crowd with her head lowered, eyes cast down, looking tired. A sailor in a cap leans against the rail at the centre right with his chin on his hand.\n\nRight: three or four people in traditional folk dress (long striped skirts, embroidered bodices, a fur-brimmed hat) stand with their backs to the viewer at the ship's rail, looking across the water. A curved ventilator and a wooden bench are at the bottom right.\n\nBackground: across the water at the upper right, the Statue of Liberty stands tall on its large pedestal and island, its arm raised with the torch, lit pale against a sky of heavy clouds that darken towards the left. Small sailing ships (schooners) are on the water in the middle distance, and a low shoreline is at the horizon.\n\nComposition: nearly all of the gazes and the sailor's pointing arm form a strong diagonal from the lower-left crowd up to the statue at the upper right, which is the lightest, most open part of the picture. The crowd is dark and dense; the statue and sky are lighter. The only words are the printed caption beneath the picture (not part of the drawing): 'Welcome to the land of freedom - An ocean steamer passing the Statue of Liberty: Scene on the steerage deck / from a sketch by a staff artist, 1887.'",
  "km25-t1": "A portrait-format magazine cover in a painterly gouache style. At the top, in hand-lettered pale cream capitals, is the masthead THE NEW YORKER ('THE' above 'NEW YORKER'), set across a grey-blue Manhattan Bridge. There is no price, date or artist credit in the picture.\n\nThe scene is a narrow city street between tall red-brick buildings: rust-red and orange walls with tall narrow windows and metal fire escapes on the right, a tan building at the left edge. The street runs away to the Manhattan Bridge, a grey-blue steel arch spanning the end of the street with a distant skyscraper visible through it. The pavement is slate blue-grey with patches of sunlit white. Parked cars are only faintly sketched along both kerbs.\n\nThe street is crowded with dozens of people, mostly fashionably dressed young women, posing, dancing, striding and crouching. Many hold cameras or smartphones pointed at others. In the foreground left a woman with a blonde ponytail in a grey jacket crouches and photographs towards the centre; at the bottom centre a woman in a yellow jacket and white lace top holds a coffee cup and crouches; at the bottom right a woman in a red top kneels with a coffee cup; in the foreground right a man in dark clothing and a cap crouches with a large camera, and at the right edge another man in dark clothes stands behind a camera on a tripod. In the middle, a woman in a pink polka-dot dress and hat dances with a coffee cup; a woman in a green-and-grey check jacket and ripped white jeans lifts a phone to photograph; a woman with red hair in a red coat and tall boots strides; another in a yellow outfit dances towards the back, and a woman in a brown hat and yellow top carries shopping bags. Behind them, many smaller figures recede towards the bridge.\n\nColour and composition: the setting is cool grey-blue and brick red, while the people are painted in bright pinks, yellows and reds that stand out against it. The converging street and the bridge lead the eye to the distance, and the photographers in the foreground face the performers in the centre, so almost every person is either posing or photographing.",
  "jr24-t1": "A portrait-format cartoon in flat, slightly textured colours on a warm mustard-yellow background, with no words anywhere in it. The scene is symmetrical: two men in identical brown suits, cream shirts and ties stand facing each other in profile at the bottom of the picture and shake hands in the centre, the handshake drawn with a few small motion marks.\n\nEach man has a plant instead of a head. The man on the left has a flowerpot where his head should be, with a thin green stem and a single red rose growing out of it. The man on the right has a tall, spiny green cactus as his head, with a small pink flower at its top.\n\nAbove each man a round thought bubble, linked to his head by two small circles, contains a picture of a plant. Above the rose-headed man the bubble shows a small round green cactus covered in spines with a pink-and-white flower on top. Above the cactus-headed man the bubble shows a long, thin, thorny green stem with a small red rose bud at its very top, against a pale background.\n\nColour and composition: the two figures mirror each other around the central handshake. The brown suits and yellow ground are muted, so the red rose and the pink flower are the only bright accents. Each bubble shows the kind of plant the other man has for a head, but drawn from its other side: the spiky cactus is shown with its flower, and the rose is shown as a thorny stem with only a tiny bud.",
  "gw25-t1": "A portrait-format book cover painted in a warm, textured style. The whole background is a mottled orange, with deeper red-orange and yellow-ochre washes (a pale ochre band runs across the lower middle). Scattered over it are concentric circles in lighter orange and red, in the manner of Aboriginal art, and small pale marks near the top left.\n\nDown the left edge stands a tall dark silhouette of a figure in profile, painted in thick brown-black brushstrokes: a person in a wide-brimmed hat, shoulders and long legs, facing to the right, standing close to the edge of the cover with a faint ground line at the feet. A dark strip like a book spine runs down the left side.\n\nAcross the upper right, in large black hand-lettered capitals and lower case, is the title WE COME WITH THIS PLACE, set over three lines ('We come' / 'with this' / 'place'). Below it, in a typewriter-style typeface, is the author's name, Debra Dank. At the top right are three round award medallions: a gold one reading 'ALS Gold Medal Winner 2023', a red one for a 2023 'Book of the Year' award, and a green one for the 2023 New South Wales Premier's Literary Awards.\n\nIn the lower half a thin black line wanders across the cover like a map: lines link a number of small hollow circles (nodes) that branch and join near the bottom centre, and one line starts near the top left and ends in a circle. These resemble tracks, a songline or a map of waterholes. At the bottom right, in the typewriter typeface, is the endorsement 'will surprise, delight and astound you' - Melissa Lucashenko.\n\nColour and composition: the figure and the line-and-circle network are the darkest elements and stand out against the warm ground; the figure's position on the left, facing into the cover, leads the eye across the title to the network of tracks.",
  "fs26-t1": "A portrait-format realistic painting in warm, dark tones, seen from a high angle looking down into a living room at night.\n\nTop left: a grey stone fireplace with a chimney breast and a bright fire burning in the hearth; the firelight is the main source of warm gold light. A wing armchair in brown fabric sits at the left edge, facing the fire. At top centre a tall window shows a dark blue, snowy winter night with bare trees. At top right a second window has a pale blind. The walls are dark russet-brown.\n\nTop right: a person with dark hair sleeps on a russet-red sofa, curled on her side with one arm under her head, covered by an olive-green blanket; an open book lies beside her. She is small in the frame, calm and still.\n\nThe foreground is a large cream and pale-green rug with an ornate scrolling floral pattern. Lower down, the rug's scrolls gradually turn into real branches and leaves, so that the floor becomes a spring garden seen from above: trees with fresh yellow-green blossom and foliage, clipped green hedges, curving red-brown paths, and at the bottom centre a stone fountain with a blue pool and a thin jet of water. The garden fills the lower half and the bottom right; the transformation is continuous, with no border between rug and garden. A faint artist signature (Gonsalves) is at the bottom right. Printed under the image on the page is the credit 'The Weaving of a Spring Dream by Rob Gonsalves'.\n\nColour and mood: warm golds and russets at the top (fire, sofa, walls) against the cool blue of the winter window, then fresh greens and yellows in the garden. The composition is a diagonal from the sleeper at top right to the fountain at the bottom, so the eye travels from the real room to the dream garden. There are no other people and no words in the picture itself.",
  "db26-t1": "A wide, panoramic, softly rendered watercolour-style illustration of a beach on a sunny day, with a pale blue sky across the top and pale sand below. The palette is soft pastel blues and whites with a few saturated accents (red and blue spades, a yellow shirt, a green bucket).\n\nRight of centre is a boy of about ten with curly blond hair and a yellow T-shirt, lying on his stomach on a striped beach towel (purple, blue, turquoise and black bands) with his bare feet up behind him. He holds a large round magnifying glass up to his right eye, which makes that eye look huge, and he is looking intently at a tiny creature (a small insect-like animal) sitting on the open palm of his other hand. His mouth is slightly pursed in concentration. Next to him on the towel are a green plastic bucket and a small ornate metal box with a latch.\n\nLeft of him, on the towel and sand, lie the tools of an explorer: black binoculars, a clear zip-lock bag holding a silver microscope, a large cream canvas bag with a handle, a yellow fishing net standing upright with a green snorkel beside it, and two spades (one blue, one red) stuck upright in the sand. Behind these, at the far left under a turquoise-and-white striped beach umbrella, two people sit in deckchairs: one wears a flat cap and reads a book or newspaper held up in front of him; the other is mostly hidden. They face away from the boy and take no notice of him. At the far right in the distance there is an elaborate sandcastle with towers, and a strip of blue sea with small waves at the horizon.\n\nComposition: the objects lead the eye left to right along the towel to the boy, whose enlarged eye is the focal point; the adults sit small and passive in the background. The scene is bright, open and uncluttered. There are no words in the image apart from tiny illegible lettering on the zip-lock bag. Printed under the image on the page is the credit 'David Wiesner, from Flotsam'.",
  "ct24-t1": "Two painted cartoon panels placed side by side, each in portrait format and separated by a thin white vertical line. Both are watercolour-and-ink illustrations showing a robot and a boy who are in the same place but doing different things.\n\nLeft panel (cool, chalky blue-grey, like a dark pavement): a hopscotch grid drawn in white chalk runs from the bottom-left corner diagonally up towards the centre, with a small pale stone lying in one of the squares. A silver-and-white humanoid robot with a boxy head, an orange-red screen for a face and jointed limbs is caught mid-hop on one leg at the top of the grid, wearing small red-and-white sneakers, so it is playing hopscotch. At the top right, apart from the robot, a boy with messy orange-brown hair, glasses, a yellow shirt and dungarees sits sideways in a green park chair with his back half-turned. He is looking down at a small handheld screen that he holds in both hands, and a pale glow around him suggests the screen is his only light. He is not looking at the game.\n\nRight panel (dull olive-grey background with dark brush streaks and ink splatter in the upper right): a white ornate park bench. On the left of the bench a large silver-blue robot with a boxy head and a blue screen face sits upright with an open picture book on its knees; the book's cover shows a small girl in a red hooded cloak against green woods and there is a small creature in the illustration. A small white tissue-like shape with tiny motion marks sits near the robot's face. On the right of the bench, shoulder to shoulder with the robot, a freckled boy with orange hair and round glasses, in a grey shirt and dungarees, sits hunched and looks down at a smartphone held in both hands, his face lit by its glow. A dark smudge hangs over his head. The robot and the boy do not look at each other.\n\nAt the bottom right of the right panel is a small blue-and-white logo reading BOOKTOON with smaller text 'Book International Cartoon Biennial'; at the bottom left is the artist's signature and '18'. The credit printed below the images is JIET KUTSANA.\n\nColour and composition: the machines are drawn in bright, detailed silver and blue and are shown moving or reading, while the boys are drawn small, hunched or turned away and lit by their screens. The same arrangement is repeated in both panels: the robot engages in a traditional activity (a chalk game, a printed storybook) while the boy beside or near it is absorbed in a device.",
  "ct23-t1": "A square colour artwork that places a modern photographed woman into a 19th-century oil painting. The painting is a crowded outdoor luncheon on a terrace, in soft, visible brushwork, under a striped orange-and-cream awning, with leafy green and golden trees behind the table and a pale patch of water with a white sail at the top right. A printed credit names the artist and says the work is an appropriation of a painting by Renoir. There are no words in the picture itself.\n\nIn the exact centre a young woman stands upright and still behind the table, looking straight out at the viewer with a blank, serious expression. She has dark wavy shoulder-length hair under a black bucket hat with a small white logo. She wears a black camisole-style dress with thin straps, a white chevron stripe across the chest and an orange-and-black tiger-striped panel on the skirt, and a long black-and-white cord hangs down her front like a necklace. Her image is sharp and flat-lit, in contrast to the soft painterly look of everything around her.\n\nIn front of her the white table is crowded with dark wine bottles, a carafe, glasses of wine, plates and a bowl of grapes and fruit. Around the table the painted people wear 1880s clothes and are shown in relaxed, social poses. At the left a man in a white sleeveless vest leans on the table and a woman with reddish hair and a lace collar sits and looks up and to the left; behind them a woman in a flowered hat lifts a glass to her lips, and a man in a top hat and a man in a flat cap stand talking at the back. At the right a large bearded man in a straw hat and a white vest stands, and a woman in a straw hat with red flowers and a dark jacket leans in to a small dog on the table. The painted figures are turned towards one another or away to the sides; none of them looks at the woman in the centre.\n\nColour and composition: the scene is warm, full of oranges, golds, reds and creamy whites, while the central woman's black, white and orange-black outfit and her upright pose make her the one modern, vertical, sharply lit element in a crowd of seated or leaning figures.",
  "bk26-t1": "A wide, realistic painting in a muted palette of greys and greens with small touches of pink and yellow, showing a sunny suburban street in the 1960s.\n\nLeft of centre, on the pavement, stand two Black children close together: an older boy of about ten in a white sweatshirt, plaid shorts and canvas sneakers, holding a baseball glove, and beside him a younger girl in a pink dress with a pink hair bow and black shoes, holding a white kitten. They face right with serious, watchful expressions. Behind them the open back of a moving van has a ramp with a dark wardrobe-style trunk on it, held by a chain; the head and cap of a mover show over the top. In the left foreground their belongings sit on the grass: part of a yellow armchair, a lamp with a cream shade and a framed picture. Behind them are neat houses with steps, a green front door and a yellow car parked on the street.\n\nRight of centre, three white children stand in a row beside the large grey-silver van, facing the new arrivals: a boy in a yellow sweater holding a baseball glove, a boy in a white baseball cap and white uniform with blue-striped socks who keeps his hands behind his back (a glove in one hand), and a girl in a grey cardigan with a ponytail, also with her hands behind her back. A black-and-tan dog sits at their feet near the middle of the scene. The van's side carries partial lettering in teal ('Mo...' and 'Co...') and small stencilled numbers near the wheel; the lettering is cut off by the frame.\n\nComposition: the two groups face each other from opposite sides of the frame, with a gap of empty pavement between them, and the big van dominates the upper right. No one smiles or speaks; every child looks at the other group. The scene is the moment before the children meet. Printed under the image on the page is the credit 'Norman Rockwell, New Kids in the Neighbourhood, 1967'.",
  "ab25-t2": "A portrait-format magazine cover in a bold, flat-colour illustrated style with strong outlines, in saturated greens, blues, yellows and pinks. At the top, in large white capital letters across the upper third, is the masthead 'THE NEW YORKER' (the words 'THE' above 'NEW YORKER', the lettering partly overlapped by tree branches). Small text at top left reads 'PRICE $8.99' and at top right 'SEPT. 9, 2024'. The artist's signature 'KIKUO' is at the lower right. A narrow black strip runs down the left edge.\n\nThe scene is a children's playground in a city park in summer, with dense green trees and a pale yellow apartment building behind. On the left is a blue climbing frame with railings and a teal slide; a child with dark curly hair in an orange top is at the top of the slide with one arm raised, seen from behind. A small girl with long wavy red hair, a pink helmet with a heart, a pink tutu skirt and rainbow leggings runs towards the slide with her back to the viewer.\n\nIn the centre foreground a woman with brown skin and dark hair in a ponytail, glasses and gold earrings stands seen mostly from behind and in profile, wearing a yellow blouse with scalloped trim, dark teal trousers and beige flat shoes, with a black shoulder bag; she holds a pink kick scooter and smiles, looking down and to the right. To the right a woman with dark brown skin and short curly black hair, in a blue-and-white striped top, dark trousers and blue sneakers, sits on a green park bench. A pale-skinned toddler with blond hair, a yellow polo shirt and olive trousers sits on her lap sucking his fingers. She holds up a smartphone towards the standing woman, and looks up at her with a warm, slightly teary smile. The phone screen is the brightest, most colourful small detail: it shows a young man smiling in a graduation cap (mortarboard) and gown. At the far right edge are the wheels of a stroller.\n\nComposition and mood: the two women are in the middle of the frame, in full colour and facing each other; the children are turned away, partly hidden, or in motion at the edges. The mood is warm and cheerful on the surface. The cover carries no caption naming the women as nannies; the question assumes that they are.",
  "ab23-t1": "A portrait-format comic-style illustration made of a green title banner and a grid of twelve square panels in four rows of three, drawn in a simple ink-outline style with flat colour. The banner across the top is bright green with the title in white capitals: HOW TO GROW IMAGINATION. Each panel has a hand-lettered caption in capitals at the top. The panels are mostly white, grey and pale blue with warm orange and yellow accents. The same girl, with brown hair and a green-and-white striped top, appears in almost every panel, and a small plant with orange-red leaves is in every panel.\n\nRow one. Panel 1, caption FIND A SPOT WHERE IT CAN GROW: the girl stands by a window holding a small seedling with orange-red leaves; a yellow rectangle of sunlight lies on the floor. Panel 2, DIG A BIG ENOUGH HOLE: she digs with a shovel beside a mound of earth, with a small green bird perched on the mound and a seedling beside her. Panel 3, WATER IT IN: standing on a stool she tips a bucket of water over a sapling growing in a pool of water, with a fish and a green bird nearby.\n\nRow two. Panel 4, TAKE TIME TO WONDER: the girl lies on her back on the ground reading, beside a sapling whose branches carry open books like leaves, with a green bird; stacks of books lie on the floor and fill the window shelf. Panel 5, MAKE SURE IT GETS PLENTY OF MOONLIGHT: a dark blue night panel in which the girl sleeps in a bed with a green striped cover, a bare-branched sapling with orange-red leaves stands next to the window, and yellow moonlight falls on the floor. Panel 6, PRETEND TO IT EVERY DAY: she wears a hat and dances or reaches towards the sapling, which is taller and has a hat hanging on it, with a green bird at her feet.\n\nRow three. Panel 7, WELCOME NEW IDEAS: the tree is now large and full of life, with many colourful birds, a squirrel, butterflies and a fish among its branches; the girl stands on a stack of boxes at the window reaching up. Panel 8, SHARE IT WITH OTHERS: the girl leans out of a window of a blue house and drops seeds or acorns to a group of children and a man in a hat and tie on a bicycle, who reach up to catch them. Panel 9, GROW A FOREST!: a green landscape of many trees and palms around a small blue house, with people, a bicycle and animals among them and an orange tree standing out.\n\nRow four. Panel 10, TRAVEL TO NEW PLACES: a yellow school bus numbered 143 full of children passes orange and green trees on a white ground. Panel 11, KEEP A PIECE OF IT WITH YOU: a rainy blue-grey panel in which children carry umbrellas and small saplings down some steps. Panel 12, FIND A NEW SPOT WHERE IT CAN GROW: in a classroom, the girl sits at a desk with a sapling in a pot and a green bird, among other students, in a patch of yellow light.\n\nThe credit printed under the illustration reads: The Shape of Ideas, GRANT SNIDER.\n\nColour and composition: the grid is regular, and the instructions run in order from the first panel to the last, with the last panel returning to the first panel's idea of finding a place for the plant to grow. The sapling's orange-red leaves are the strongest colour against the muted blue, grey and white, and the plant grows larger and more crowded with creatures from panel to panel.",
  "ab23-t4": "A landscape-format figure: a thin black rectangle divided into two equal panels side by side, with a short italic note printed above it and the page numbers p.283 and p.284 printed under the two panels. The note above reads: NB: The following pages surround the events of September 11th 2001 in the World Trade Centre (p.283 – 284). The credit under the box reads JONATHAN SAFRAN FOER, and the heading above reads from Extremely Loud and Incredibly Close.\n\nLeft panel (p.283): a white page of black serif text in a paragraph with generous white space around it. In order, it reads:\n\"But this is the thing I have never told anyone. I looked at the caller ID and saw that it was his cell phone. I couldn't pick up the phone. I just couldn't do it. It rang and rang, and I couldn't move. I wanted to pick it up, but I couldn't. He needed me, and I couldn't pick up. The answering machine went on, and I heard my own voice.\"\nThen, in italics and indented: Hi, you've reached the Schell residence. Please leave a message.\nThen: \"There was a beep. Then I heard Dad's voice.\"\nThen, in italics and indented, four lines: Are you there? Are you there? Are you there? Are you there? / Are you there? Are you there? Are you there? Are you there? / Are you there? Are you there? Are you there? / Are you there? Are you\nThen, after a gap: \"And then it cut off.\"\nThe lower half of the page is empty.\n\nRight panel (p.284): a tall, narrow rectangle filled almost edge to edge with thousands of tiny lines of black printed text that have been printed on top of one another again and again, so that the lines merge into a dense, solid black-grey texture. Thin white horizontal gaps show where the lines of type sit, giving it a woven or striped appearance, and the black is slightly thinner at the top edge. No single word or letter can be read anywhere on this page. There is a narrow white margin round it.\n\nColour and composition: the page on the left is mostly white and readable, while the page on the right is almost entirely black and unreadable, and the two sit directly side by side so that the reader moves from the sparse spoken words to the solid black block after the line \"And then it cut off.\"",
  "fs25-t5": "A black-and-white pen-and-ink graphic novel page in two panels, one above the other. All lettering is hand-drawn in capitals; it is given here in sentence case.\n\nTop panel (the larger): a snowy forest of thin spruce trees. In the centre is a close-up of an older man's head and shoulders: short pale hair, dark-rimmed glasses, a pale shirt, mouth slightly open as if speaking. Across the foreground a team of husky sled dogs, joined by harness lines, runs in a curve from the left edge past the man towards the right; the dog at the left has its mouth open and tongue out. To the right of the man a young woman with a dark bob haircut stands beside a partly shown figure in a hood who raises a hand. At the top right a person in a fur-trimmed parka rides a dogsled along a snowy track. The figures overlap with no borders between them.\nCaption boxes in the top panel read: 'In land-claim talks, each region or community must negotiate its territorial area – called land quantum – and settle its surface and subsurface mineral rights.' 'I ask Jim how the Dene reconcile the legalistic, Euro-Western concept of land with their own worldview.' 'He wonders whether I've seen maps showing the enormous area Dene trappers and hunters traversed even in the time of the dogsleds.' Speech balloons in the top panel read: 'My old grandmother, when I was very young, she told me one time,' 'Don't ever let them put a fence around us.' 'It's like a spiderweb…' 'Just an example of how much our land was used…'\n\nBottom panel: an indoor scene, densely cross-hatched, with a dark hatched wall behind. Four middle-aged and older men in dark suits and ties lean over a table covered with a map. From the left: a frowning man holds a pair of compass dividers on the map; behind him a bald man points at the map; a bearded man in glasses holds up a clear protractor; at the far right an older man, partly shown, rests his hand at his chin. A calculator lies at the lower left of the table. Speech balloons in the bottom panel read: 'But when you talk about quantum… and surface and subsurface,' 'That's the language that the Fed negotiators have put on the table…' 'And it's a concept that is totally foreign to us, but we have to educate ourselves on it and see what happens.' The page number 78 is printed at the bottom of the panel.\n\nPrinted beneath the page: 'Note: This extract, from Joe Sacco's Paying the Land, describes land rights negotiations between the Dene people (indigenous to the Arctic North-West of North America) and the Federal Government of Canada.' The credit is: JOE SACCO.",
  "py25-t5": "A colour photograph, in landscape format, taken at eye level, of a very dense crowd of people queuing outdoors in winter, so tightly packed that bodies overlap and fill the whole frame. Almost everyone wears dark or muted winter clothing: hoods, beanies, a pale beige hood, a white baseball cap, dark coats, a teal jacket, scarves. Their faces are turned in many directions; several people in the foreground have their backs to the camera, dark hair and shoulders filling the bottom of the picture.\n\nAt the centre, a woman with short, pale blonde hair and no hat, in a dark coat, is the brightest point in the picture. She is in sharper focus than the people around her and stands out from the crowd; she looks calmly, slightly to the right of the camera, with a still, composed expression. Immediately to her left (the viewer's right) a man in a beige hood and glasses looks forward; behind them a young man in dark sunglasses faces the camera; to the right of him in the background a bearded man in a dark hoodie looks out of the frame; in the right foreground a man in a white cap looks down and to the left. At the top right a woman in a rust-coloured jacket raises one arm. Behind the crowd are a stone wall (top right) and blurred buildings and street lights in cool, hazy light.\n\nThe colours are muted: greys, navy, beige and brown, with a few spots of colour (a mustard beanie, a rust jacket, a teal jacket). The depth is shallow: the foreground and edges are soft, the centre is sharper. No text appears in the picture itself. The printed caption beneath the image reads: 'Yulia Navalnaya, centre, widow of Alexey Navalny (a noted Russian anti-corruption lawyer and activist), stands in a queue with other voters at a polling station near the Russian embassy in Berlin on March 17, during the 2024 Russian presidential elections.' The credit is: EBRAHIM NAROOZI, 2024."
};
